{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**The problem.** Given a binary search tree and two of its nodes `p` and `q`, return their **lowest common ancestor** — the deepest node that has both `p` and `q` in its subtree. A node counts as its own ancestor."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/ab1f6a43-20a3-4c98-95b4-56bc500ffce8.svg",
      "alt": "The tree with root 6. For p = 3 and q = 5: both are below 6, so go left to 2; both are above 2, so go right to 4; 3 is below 4 and 5 is above it, so they split here and 4 is the answer.",
      "width": 956,
      "height": 490,
